      "text": "Since $\\sin E = 1$, $E$ must be a right angle (90 degrees) because the sine of an angle in a triangle is equal to the ratio of the length of the side opposite the angle to the length of the hypotenuse, and the only way for this ratio to be equal to 1 is if the opposite side is equal to the hypotenuse.\n\nSince $E$ is 90 degrees, $D + F = 90$ degrees because the sum of the angles in a triangle is 180 degrees.\n\nTherefore, $\\sin F = \\sin (90 - D) = \\cos D$.\n\nSince $\\sin^2 D + \\cos^2 D = 1$, we can solve for $\\cos D$:\n\n\\[\n\\cos^2 D = 1 - \\sin^2 D = 1 - \\left(\\frac{5}{13}\\right)^2 = 1 - \\frac{25}{169} = \\frac{144}{169}\n\\]\n\nSo $\\cos D = \\frac{12}{13}$ (since $\\cos$ is positive in the first quadrant).\n\nTherefore, $\\sin F = \\cos D = \\boxed{\\frac{12}{13}}$.",
