      "text": "Given that in right triangle \\( DEF \\), we have \\(\\sin D = \\frac{5}{13}\\) and \\(\\sin E = 1\\). We need to find \\(\\sin F\\).\n\nFirst, let's determine the possible values for \\( \\sin D \\) and \\( \\sin E \\) in a right triangle context. Since \\(\\sin D = \\frac{5}{13}\\) and \\(D\\) is an angle in a right triangle, the opposite side to angle \\(D\\) is 5 and the hypotenuse is 13. Using the Pythagorean theorem, we can find the adjacent side:\n\n\\[\n\\text{Adjacent} = \\sqrt{13^2 - 5^2} = \\sqrt{169 - 25} = \\sqrt{144} = 12\n\\]\n\nSo, the opposite side to angle \\(D\\) is 5, the adjacent side to angle \\(D\\) is 12, and the hypotenuse is 13. Therefore, \\(\\cos D = \\frac{12}{13}\\).\n\nSince \\(\\sin E = 1\\), angle \\(E\\) must be \\(90^\\circ\\). This means that angle \\(F\\) is the complement of angle \\(D\\) in the right triangle \\(DEF\\). Therefore, \\(\\angle F = 90^\\circ - \\angle D\\).\n\nIn a right triangle, the sum of the angles is \\(180^\\circ\\). Since one angle is \\(90^\\circ\\), the sum of the other two angles is \\(90^\\circ\\). Hence, \\(\\angle F = 90^\\circ - \\angle D\\).\n\nUsing the sine rule for the sine of an angle in a right triangle, we know that:\n\n\\[\n\\sin F = \\sin(90^\\circ - D) = \\cos D\n\\]\n\nSince we have already calculated \\(\\cos D = \\frac{12}{13}\\), it follows that:\n\n\\[\n\\sin F = \\frac{12}{13}\n\\]\n\nThus, the value of \\(\\sin F\\) is \\(\\boxed{\\frac{12}{13}}\\).",
