      "sample_index": 3,
      "text": "We start by analyzing the given information. In the right triangle \\(DEF\\), we know that \\(\\sin E = 1\\). This implies that angle \\(E\\) is \\(90^\\circ\\) because the sine of \\(90^\\circ\\) is 1. Therefore, triangle \\(DEF\\) is part of a right triangle with one angle being \\(90^\\circ\\).\n\nGiven \\(\\sin D = \\frac{5}{13}\\), we need to determine \\(\\sin F\\). In a right triangle, the sum of the non-right angles is \\(90^\\circ\\). Hence, \\(D + E + F = 90^\\circ\\). Since \\(E = 90^\\circ\\), we have \\(D + F = 90^\\circ\\). This means \\(F = 90^\\circ - D\\).\n\nNext, we use the fact that \\(\\sin F = \\sin (90^\\circ - D) = \\cos D\\). We need to find \\(\\cos D\\). We use the Pythagorean identity for sine and cosine, which states:\n\n\\[\n\\sin^2 D + \\cos^2 D = 1\n\\]\n\nGiven \\(\\sin D = \\frac{5}{13}\\), we substitute this value into the identity:\n\n\\[\n\\left(\\frac{5}{13}\\right)^2 + \\cos^2 D = 1\n\\]\n\n\\[\n\\frac{25}{169} + \\cos^2 D = 1\n\\]\n\n\\[\n\\cos^2 D = 1 - \\frac{25}{169}\n\\]\n\n\\[\n\\cos^2 D = \\frac{169}{169} - \\frac{25}{169}\n\\]\n\n\\[\n\\cos^2 D = \\frac{144}{169}\n\\]\n\nTaking the positive square root (since \\(\\cos D\\) is positive in the first quadrant where \\(D\\) is),\n\n\\[\n\\cos D = \\frac{12}{13}\n\\]\n\nThus, \\(\\sin F = \\cos D = \\frac{12}{13}\\).\n\nThe final answer is:\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\frac{12}{13}}\n\\]",
